<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  PID control of ITMT trips, used when thermald runs in adaptive mode and
  Enable below is 1.

  An ITMT table entry gives a trip temperature for a sensor (usually a skin
  sensor) and a PL1 range. Once the sensor reaches the trip, a PID moves the
  PL1 power limit within that range so that the sensor settles near the
  trip, instead of cutting PL1 in steps and restoring it (a sawtooth). Below
  the trip, minus a 2 C hysteresis, PL1 is released back gradually.

  Each element is optional; a missing or invalid one keeps the default shown
  here. These are the built-in defaults, tuned for a common 25 W client
  system. Without this file, or if it can't be parsed, the PID is not used.
  thermald reads this file at start. Restart thermald after a change.

  How the PID acts, every 5 s while the trip is active (e: sensor
  temperature minus the set point, in millicelsius):

      PL1 change (uW) = Kp * e * seconds since the last change
                        + Kd * (change of e since then)

  Above the set point PL1 is never raised, and 1 C or more above the trip
  PL1 is cut at least as fast as the default controller would.
-->
<ItmtPid>
	<!--
	  Enable: 1 to control ITMT trips with the PID described here. 0: the
	  default controller, which cuts PL1 in steps while the sensor is above
	  the trip and releases it again once the sensor is 2 C below the trip.
	-->
	<Enable>0</Enable>

	<!--
	  Kp: how fast PL1 keeps changing while the temperature stays away from
	  the set point, in uW per millicelsius per second. 30: 30 mW per second
	  for each degree of error. Larger: reaches the limit sooner, but with
	  slow skin sensors it overshoots and oscillates more.
	-->
	<Kp>30</Kp>

	<!--
	  Kd: how much PL1 changes at once when the temperature changes, in uW
	  per millicelsius. 1000: 1 W for each degree the sensor rises or falls.
	  Larger: reacts more strongly to each change of the sensor reading.
	-->
	<Kd>1000</Kd>

	<!--
	  SetPointOffset: the PID aims this far below the ITMT trip temperature,
	  in millicelsius. 500: half a degree, so that sensors that report whole
	  degrees are about as often above the set point as below it.
	-->
	<SetPointOffset>500</SetPointOffset>

	<!--
	  ScaleWithRange: 1 to scale Kp and Kd with the PL1 range of the ITMT
	  entry (PL1 maximum minus PL1 minimum), so that a system with a larger
	  range moves through it about as fast as the reference system. 0 to use
	  Kp and Kd exactly as given.
	-->
	<ScaleWithRange>1</ScaleWithRange>

	<!--
	  ReferenceRange: the PL1 range, in mW, for which Kp and Kd are given.
	  17000: a 25 W client system limited down to 8 W. An entry with a 34 W
	  range gets twice the gains (before ScaleMax applies).
	-->
	<ReferenceRange>17000</ReferenceRange>

	<!--
	  ScaleMin, ScaleMax: limits for that scale factor. With a skin sensor
	  that responds slowly (30 s or more), gains above about 1.5 times make
	  the control oscillate, so keep ScaleMax at 1.5 unless the sensor is
	  known to respond faster.
	-->
	<ScaleMin>0.5</ScaleMin>
	<ScaleMax>1.5</ScaleMax>
</ItmtPid>
